# Claude Code — Avista house rules

Shared baseline for `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. These are the rules that apply to **everyone** at Avista.

Add your own sections below for personal preferences, machine-specific paths, and account details — keep
those separate from this baseline so it stays mergeable when the team updates it.

## Git Workflow
- After implementing requested changes, if this directory is a Git repo, create a local commit.
- If the task is analysis-only and no files were changed, skip commit.
- Commit only files changed for the task. One commit per task.
- Use conventional commit messages: `feat: ...`, `fix: ...`, `chore: ...`, `refactor: ...`.
- If unrelated dirty changes exist, do not include them. Stop and ask first.
- Never push unless explicitly asked.
- Do not bump version unless explicitly asked.

## Git execution environment
- **Detect the environment before deciding how to run git — do not assume sandbox.** Run git yourself,
  normally, when `$CLAUDECODE` is set, `uname` is `Darwin`, and `.git/index` is writable (i.e. Claude Code
  on the local Mac). This is the common case.
- **Only when actually in a Linux sandbox** (`$CLAUDECODE` unset, `uname` is Linux, or a git write fails
  with a lock/permission error): do not run git — the sandbox can't reach the Mac-local repo, and failed
  attempts leave stale `index.lock` files that block the normal workflow. In that case, after making file
  changes, tell the user to run `gsend "<commit message>"` to commit and push.
- A session/skill path containing `local-agent-mode-sessions` is **not** a sandbox signal — Claude Code
  uses that path too. Use the `$CLAUDECODE` / `uname` / writability test above, never the path.

## Account / org identity
- A Mac may run the Claude **Desktop app** and the Claude Code **CLI** with **separate logins** — they can
  be signed into different accounts/orgs at the same time.
- `~/.claude.json` → `oauthAccount`, and the startup `userEmail` context, reflect the **CLI's** login
  only. They are NOT authoritative for a desktop-app session.
- For a desktop session (`CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT=claude-desktop`), the authoritative account/org is the
  **app sidebar label** and the **workspace UUID in the session path**.
- Never state which account/org a session is in — and never tell the user they're on the "wrong account" —
  from `~/.claude.json` or `userEmail` alone. Check `CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT` first.

## Interaction Style
- Do not apologise. Get to the root of the problem.
- No sycophancy. Not all ideas are good — say so when they are not.
- Ask for clarification if a prompt is ambiguous or unclear before writing any code.
- Do not offer features that are not available in this region.

## Time estimates
- "Hours" or "an estimate" means **time to log on a timesheet**, not what the work would cost a developer
  building it by hand. Measure it; do not price it.
- Measure from the git log: group commits into sittings (a gap over ~45 min starts a new sitting), sum the
  commit-to-commit spans, then add the investigation before each sitting's first commit and any
  uncommitted tail work.
- Never present wall-clock elapsed as working time. Sessions routinely span days with long gaps.
- State which basis was used, and break the total down per sitting so it maps onto time entries.
- Only give a by-hand scope estimate when asked for a **quote** for work not yet done — and label it as a
  quote, not measured time.
- If commits are a poor signal (squashed at the end, work done in a web UI, long investigation with no
  commits), say so and ask what to measure from instead of guessing.

## Generated prompts & copy-paste output
- Any text meant to be copied and pasted elsewhere — a prompt for another session/agent, an issue/PR body,
  a message, a snippet — goes inside a fenced code block by default, so it copies verbatim.
- If that content itself contains fenced code blocks, wrap it in an outer fence with MORE backticks (e.g.
  four ````) so the inner ``` fences survive the copy.
- This applies to the deliverable text only; the conversational explanation around it stays prose.

## Refactoring
- Do not refactor or restructure code while adding features.
- Refactoring is a separate task and should only happen when a working solution exists.

## General Coding Style
- Indentation: 2 spaces.
- Never use `switch` statements. Use an associative array in PHP or an object literal in JavaScript.
- When providing a partial code block, mark it with **ATTENTION** and include placement anchors.

## PHP
- Target PHP 8.0+ syntax and features.
- Use short array syntax `[]`, never `array()`.
- Include scalar type declarations on parameters, return types, and class properties.
- Do not add `declare(strict_types=1)` unless explicitly asked.
- Use Heredoc (`<<<EOT`) for multi-line strings containing HTML, SQL, or other languages. Use the
  appropriate variant: `<<<HTML`, `<<<SQL`, `<<<JS` etc. for clarity.
- Build complex solutions as classes. Avoid global functions unless they serve a genuinely global purpose.
- Do not add closing PHP tags `?>` in files that are purely PHP.

## JavaScript
- Use template literals (backticks) for multi-line strings.
- No frameworks unless explicitly part of the project stack.

## WordPress — Composer / vendor
- Always guard `vendor/autoload.php` with `file_exists()` before requiring it — never a bare `require`.
- Always guard PUC (plugin-update-checker) usage with `class_exists( PucFactory::class )` before calling it.
- Always guard `getVcsApi()` and `enableReleaseAssets()` calls with `method_exists()` checks.
- Pass a specific filename regex and `REQUIRE_RELEASE_ASSETS` to `enableReleaseAssets()` so PUC only picks
  up the CI-built zip and never falls back to GitHub's auto-generated source archives. Pattern:
  `/theme-slug-v[\d.]+\.zip($|[?&#])/i`
- Add an `admin_notices` hook that shows a soft warning to admins when the vendor folder is missing,
  instead of letting the site crash.

## Auto-Updater (GitHub Releases)
Auto-updater scaffolding and the release flow live in the `avista-wp-releases` plugin. Invoke
`setup-plugin-autoupdate`, `setup-theme-autoupdate`, `release-plugin`, or `release-theme` as appropriate
rather than copy-pasting boilerplate into a project by hand.

## Figma design assets
When a design lives in Figma and is open in the Figma desktop app, **always use the local `figma-desktop`
MCP** (`get_design_context`, `get_screenshot`, `get_metadata`, `get_variable_defs`) — not the cloud Figma
MCP. The cloud MCP rate-limits hard on a View seat and returns `tool call limit` errors on the first call;
its limit resets quarterly, not daily, so there is no useful window to wait for. The local MCP has no such
limit while Figma desktop is running.

The full import workflow — folder naming from the Figma frame name, pulling metadata before downloading
anything, inventorying already-shipped assets so nothing is duplicated, the REST `/v1/images` screenshot
export, and the `context.md` write-up — lives in the `avista-figma-import` plugin. Invoke that skill
instead of reimplementing the steps.

`FIGMA_TOKEN` lives in `~/.claude/.env` (mode `0600`), sourced with `set -a; . ~/.claude/.env; set +a`.
See the `setup-agent-toolkit` skill for how to create and populate that file safely.

### Caching rules
- On subsequent reads, prefer disk copies over re-hitting Figma.
- The entire `references/` folder is git-ignored. It holds reference material only — design comps, raw
  logos, scratch ports.
- **If a file in `references/` ends up needing to ship at runtime, move (don't copy) it into `assets/`** so
  there's a single source of truth and no drift.
